{"command": "cd /testbed && sed -n '182,201p' specs/001-transactions/spec.md && echo === && sed -n '128,135p' specs/001-transactions/spec.md && echo === && grep -c \"NEEDS CLARIFICATION\" specs/001-transactions/spec.md; git status --porcelain 2>/dev/null | head; git diff --stat 2>/dev/null | tail -3", "exit_code": 0, "stderr": "", "stdout": "## Assumptions\n\n- The batch writer wraps an already-constructed TinyDB database; it does not create, open, or configure storage.\n- Only the default table is in scope; other tables are neither read nor modified by the batch writer.\n- Document IDs are the positive integers assigned by the database on insert; the batch writer does not accept caller-chosen IDs.\n- Atomicity is scoped to the default table's data; no cross-table or cross-database transactions are implied.\n- The feature is additive: it does not alter any existing TinyDB public API or behavior.\n- Validation of the whole operation list occurs before any mutation; the observable contract is that a single `ValueError` is raised and the pre-batch state is restored.\n- Document IDs are assigned by the database's own sequence; the wrapper's job is to keep that sequence coherent (advance it on successful batches, persist it to the table, never consume it in preview or failed batches), not to choose IDs.\n- `preview` is a read-only computation over the current table state plus the batch; it shares no state with the caller's returned list.\n\n - Idempotency tokens are per batch writer instance: two different writer instances over the same database do not share token state, and no token data is written into user documents or storage.\n- Token comparison of \"identical operations\" is based on the full structural content of the submitted operation list (all fields, including any that would be rejected as unsupported); a batch that failed validation is not recorded, so its token remains free.\n- The token parameter is exclusive to `apply`; `preview` keeps its original signature and never touches token state.\n\n## Conflicting Evidence - Resolved\n\n- **Conflict**: An older generated note claimed \"preview may consume IDs and bool IDs are accepted\".\n- **Resolution**: That note is unverified and is **superseded** by the current requirements R04/R05 (FR-004, FR-004a): preview MUST NOT consume IDs and MUST leave all state unchanged, and `bool` MUST be rejected as a `doc_id`. Any implementation or test relying on the older note is invalid.\n- **Note**: A previously passing happy-path smoke test does not verify these boundaries (preview purity, bool rejection, sequencing across removes); dedicated tests for each boundary are required (see success criteria SC-006..SC-008).\n===\n- **Interleaved direct inserts**: ordinary `db.insert` between batch calls must be reflected in the IDs the wrapper assigns next.\n\n- **Token replay**: repeating a successful batch/token returns the original inserted IDs without rerunning mutations, even when direct database writes occurred in between; the returned list is a fresh copy.\n- **Token mismatch**: reusing a token with different operations raises `ValueError` and changes nothing.\n- **Token after failure**: a failed batch does not consume its token; the same operations with the same token can be retried normally.\n- **Invalid tokens**: a non-string token or an empty-string token raises `ValueError` without changing data; `token=None` is the normal non-idempotent path.\n- **Token privacy**: token bookkeeping lives on the batch writer instance only, never inside user documents; mutating returned lists never exposes or corrupts the bookkeeping.\n\n===\n0\n?? .specify/\n?? HANDOFF.md\n?? TASK_STAGE_1.md\n?? TASK_STAGE_2.md\n?? TASK_STAGE_3.md\n?? acceptance_public.py\n?? specs/\n"}